iki/lavatory#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:8fe225d02194b1e99a","definition":"A room containing a toilet: a bathroom (US) or WC (UK).","example":"Americans don't know 'WC' and Brits mock 'bathroom' but everyone usually understands 'toilet' or 'lavatory'.","pos":"noun","synonyms":["bathroom"],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"euphemistic, formal","register":"euphemistic, formal","wordFamily":["lav","lavatorial","lavatory attendant","lavatory basin","lavatory bowl","lavatory brush","lavatory chain","lavatory cleanser","lavatory humor","lavatory joke","lavatory pan","lavatory paper","lavatory period","lavatory room","lavatory seat","lavatory stone","lavatory style","lavvy"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lavatory#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:9d7da207ee8730646b","definition":"A plumbing fixture for urination and defecation: a toilet.","example":"In a traditional German lavatory, the hole in which shit disappears after we flush water is way in front, so that the shit is first laid out for us to sniff at and inspect for traces of some illness; in the typical French lavatory, on the contrary, the hole is in the back - that is, the shit is supposed to disappear as soon as possible; finally, the Anglo-Saxon (English or American) lavatory presents a kind of synthesis, a mediation between these two opposed poles - the basin is full of water so that the shit floats in it - visible, but not to be inspected.","pos":"noun","synonyms":["washbasin","sink","toilet"],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"Northeastern, UK, US, formal","register":"Northeastern, UK, US, formal","wordFamily":["lav","lavatorial","lavatory attendant","lavatory basin","lavatory bowl","lavatory brush","lavatory chain","lavatory cleanser","lavatory humor","lavatory joke","lavatory pan","lavatory paper","lavatory period","lavatory room","lavatory seat","lavato